<h2 dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"125:1-125:18">
<a id=3D"user-content-amazon-dynamodb" class=3D"anchor" aria-hidden=3D"true=
" href=3D"https://git.epam.com/epmc-acm-public/aws-associate-training/-/blo=
b/master/materials/database_services/README.md#amazon-dynamodb"></a>Amazon =
DynamoDB</h2>
<p dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"127:1-127:36"><a rel=3D"noopener noreferr=
er" href=3D"https://git.epam.com/epmc-acm-public/aws-associate-training/-/r=
aw/master/materials/database_services/images/aws-dynamodb-console.jpg" clas=
s=3D"no-attachment-icon gfm" target=3D"_blank"><img class=3D"gfm js-lazy-lo=
aded" decoding=3D"async" alt=3D"" src=3D"https://git.epam.com/epmc-acm-publ=
ic/aws-associate-training/-/raw/master/materials/database_services/images/a=
ws-dynamodb-console.jpg" data-sourcepos=3D"127:1-127:36" loading=3D"lazy" d=
ata-testid=3D"js-lazy-loaded-content"></a></p>
<p dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"129:1-129:171"><a rel=3D"nofollow norefer=
rer noopener" href=3D"https://aws.amazon.com/dynamodb/" data-sourcepos=3D"1=
29:1-129:51" target=3D"_blank">Amazon DynamoDB</a> is a fully managed <stro=
ng data-sourcepos=3D"129:72-129:80">NoSQL</strong> database service that pr=
ovides fast and predictable performance with seamless scalability.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"131:1-131:176">In DynamoDB, tables, items=
, and attributes are the core components that you work with. A=E2=80=AFtabl=
e=E2=80=AFis a collection of=E2=80=AFitems, and each item is a collection o=
f=E2=80=AFattributes.=E2=80=AF</p>
<p dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"133:1-133:230">DynamoDB supports=E2=80=AF=
eventually consistent=E2=80=AFand=E2=80=AFstrongly consistent=E2=80=AFreads=
. For more information on that, see <a rel=3D"nofollow noreferrer noopener"=
 href=3D"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/H=
owItWorks.ReadConsistency.html" data-sourcepos=3D"133:114-133:229" target=
=3D"_blank">Read Consistency</a>.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"135:1-135:224">DynamoDB comes in two <a r=
el=3D"nofollow noreferrer noopener" href=3D"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ama=
zondynamodb/latest/developerguide/HowItWorks.ReadWriteCapacityMode.html" da=
ta-sourcepos=3D"135:23-135:153" target=3D"_blank">Read/Write capacity modes=
</a>, which should be chosen depending on application load and your budget.=
</p>
<p dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"137:1-137:150">For working with data, you=
 can access Amazon DynamoDB using the AWS Management Console, the AWS Comma=
nd Line Interface (AWS CLI), or the DynamoDB API.</p>
<h3 dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"139:1-139:30">
<a id=3D"user-content-use-cases--considerations-1" class=3D"anchor" aria-hi=
dden=3D"true" href=3D"https://git.epam.com/epmc-acm-public/aws-associate-tr=
aining/-/blob/master/materials/database_services/README.md#use-cases--consi=
derations-1"></a>Use cases / Considerations</h3>
<p dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"141:1-141:33"><a rel=3D"noopener noreferr=
er" href=3D"https://git.epam.com/epmc-acm-public/aws-associate-training/-/r=
aw/master/materials/database_services/images/no-sql-simple-app.png" class=
=3D"no-attachment-icon gfm" target=3D"_blank"><img class=3D"gfm js-lazy-loa=
ded" decoding=3D"async" alt=3D"" src=3D"https://git.epam.com/epmc-acm-publi=
c/aws-associate-training/-/raw/master/materials/database_services/images/no=
-sql-simple-app.png" data-sourcepos=3D"141:1-141:33" loading=3D"lazy" data-=
testid=3D"js-lazy-loaded-content"></a></p>
<p dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"143:1-143:163">Whenever SQL database (lik=
e RDS) is not suitable for your needs (e.g., data structure is not the same=
 across all items), DynamoDB is a great choice for such cases.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"145:1-145:253">If your application requir=
es really fast reads (real-time bidding, social gaming, and trading applica=
tions) DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) is a good choice, as it will cache query =
data from DynamoDB and your application will get the needed data much faste=
r.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"147:1-147:124">As DynamoDB has two Read/W=
rite capacity modes, it=E2=80=99s important to understand, whether to use O=
n-Demand or Provisioned mode.</p>
<h3 dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"149:1-149:14">
<a id=3D"user-content-governance" class=3D"anchor" aria-hidden=3D"true" hre=
f=3D"https://git.epam.com/epmc-acm-public/aws-associate-training/-/blob/mas=
ter/materials/database_services/README.md#governance"></a>Governance</h3>
<p dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"151:1-151:239">DynamoDB provides on-deman=
d backup capability. It allows you to create full backups of your tables fo=
r long-term retention and archival for regulatory compliance needs. For mor=
e information, see=E2=80=AFOn-Demand Backup and Restore for DynamoDB.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"153:1-153:357">You can create on-demand b=
ackups and enable point-in-time recovery for your Amazon DynamoDB tables. P=
oint-in-time recovery helps protect your tables from accidental write or de=
lete operations. With point-in-time recovery, you can restore a table to an=
y point in time during the last 35 days. For more information, see=E2=80=AF=
Point-in-Time Recovery: How It Works.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"155:1-155:240">DynamoDB allows you to del=
ete expired items from tables automatically to help you reduce storage usag=
e and the cost of storing data that is no longer relevant. For more informa=
tion, see=E2=80=AFExpiring Items By Using DynamoDB Time to Live (TTL).</p>
<p dir=3D"auto" data-sourcepos=3D"157:1-157:89">Dynamo is also integrated w=
ith CloudWatch, allowing you to enable logging and monitoring.</p>
